This is a great example where a Context-based approach can be extremely useful for the Tooltip component.
The idea is to have a provider that wraps the application, and then uses a shared state to keep track of the active tooltip (if any).

Step 1: Create the context

Let's create a new file called Context/TooltipContext.tsx:

import React from 'react';
// Define the type of the tooltip item
type TooltipItem = {
element: React.ReactNode; // The element to be displayed as a tooltip
};
// Create a context with no initial value
const TooltipContext = React.createContext<{
isOpen: boolean; // To keep track of the tooltip state
close: () => void; // A function to close the tooltip
open: (tooltipItem: TooltipItem) => void; // A function to open a new tooltip
}>({
isOpen: false,
close: () => {},
open: () => {},
});
export default TooltipContext;

Step 2: Create the provider

Create a new file called Context/TooltipProvider.tsx:

import React, { ReactNode, useState } from 'react';
import TooltipContext from './TooltipContext';
type Props = {
children: ReactNode;
};
export default function TooltipProvider({ children }: Props) {
const [activeTooltipItem, setActiveTooltipItem] = useState<{ element: React.ReactNode } | null>(null);
// Function to close the tooltip
function close() {
setActiveTooltipItem(null);
}
// Function to open a tooltip
function open(tooltipItem: { element: React.ReactNode }) {
// If there is already an active tooltip, close it first
if (activeTooltipItem) {
setActiveTooltipItem(null);
}
// Set the new tooltip item as active
setActiveTooltipItem(tooltipItem);
}
return (
<TooltipContext.Provider value={{ isOpen: !!activeTooltipItem, close, open }}>
{children}
</TooltipContext.Provider>
);
}

Step 3: Create the tooltip component

Finally, let's create our Tooltip component that will be used to display the tooltip when needed. Create a new file called Components/Tooltip.tsx:

import React, { ReactNode } from 'react';
import TooltipContext from '../Context/TooltipContext';
type Props = {
children: ReactNode;
element: React.ReactNode;
className?: string;
};
export default function Tooltip({ children, element, className }: Props) {
const { open } = React.useContext(TooltipContext);
const [tooltipVisible, setTooltipVisible] = React.useState(false);
function onMouseEnter() {
open({ element });
setTooltipVisible(true);
}
return (
<>
<div
className={className}
onMouseEnter={onMouseEnter}
onMouseLeave={() => setTooltipVisible(false)}
onFocus={() => setTooltipVisible(true)}
onBlur={() => setTooltipVisible(false)}
>
{children}
</div>
{tooltipVisible && <div className="tooltip">{element}</div>}
</>
);
}

Conclusion

The code we've created allows us to display Tooltips across an application that are mutually exclusive. This means only one Tooltip can be open at a time and any previous Tooltip is closed automatically when a new one is opened.

The implementation uses the Context API and a Provider pattern to keep track of the state of the Tooltips and share it across the application. This is useful when the components that display the Tooltips are located in different places but need to share state.

By using this pattern, we ensure a better and consistent user experience since there will always be only one Tooltip visible at a time, and any previous one will be automatically closed.

In this example, we have a Provider that manages the state of the open tooltip and functions to open and close it. The Tooltip component consumes this state through the context and displays the corresponding tooltip element when activated.

