Here is the implementation of a layout component using **React** and **Tailwind CSS**.

Since Tailwind handles styling via utility classes, the core logic is simply passing a `className` prop that switches between the two desired layout styles.

### The Component (`Layout.tsx`)

This component acts as a wrapper. It accepts `children` (your page content) and a `variant` prop to determine the layout width.

```tsx
import React from 'react';

type LayoutVariant = 'full-width' | 'centered';

interface LayoutProps {
  children: React.ReactNode;
  variant?: LayoutVariant;
}

const Layout: React.FC<LayoutProps> = ({ children, variant = 'full-width' }) => {
  // Define the classes based on the variant
  const containerClasses = {
    'full-width': 'w-full px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8 py-8',
    'centered': 'max-w-7xl mx-auto w-full px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8 py-8',
  }[variant];

  return (
    <main className={containerClasses}>
      {children}
    </main>
  );
};

export default Layout;
```

### How to Use It

You can use this component in your page routes or templates.

#### 1. Full Width Variant
This removes the max-width constraint, allowing content to span the entire viewport width (minus the padding).

```tsx
import Layout from './Layout';

const FullWidthPage = () => (
  <Layout variant="full-width">
    <h1 className="text-3xl font-bold">Full Width Content</h1>
    <p>This content goes edge to edge (with padding).</p>
  </Layout>
);
```

#### 2. Centered Variant
This adds a `max-w-7xl` (or whatever width you choose) and `mx-auto` to center the content on the screen.

```tsx
import Layout from './Layout';

const CenteredPage = () => (
  <Layout variant="centered">
    <h1 className="text-3xl font-bold">Centered Content</h1>
    <p>This content stays within a specific width and centers itself.</p>
  </Layout>
);
```

